# Bracha 1987, "Asynchronous Byzantine Agreement Protocols"
# Information and Computation 75, 130-143 (1987)
#
# Figure 1, BPR's replies: what a (ready, v) arriving without the
# RECEIVED annotation does, and what p's (initial, v) or BESIDE
# arriving at a process that holds both does.  Two rows, in their own
# file because their inputs touch nothing in bracha87Fig1.dtc: compiled
# with them, dtc carries them down every path of the figure's dispatch
# for two leaves that read them.  Compiled alone it is a few lines, run
# once per event after the main dispatch, as bracha87Fig3.dtc runs
# after Figure 1's.
#
# The annotations are the caller's, riding the (ready, v) BPR already
# re-sends (bracha87Fig1.dtc, BPR's rows): ACCEPTED, the sender has
# accepted, and RECEIVED, set per recipient, the sender holds that
# recipient's accept, a (ready, v) from it carrying ACCEPTED.  A
# (ready, v) carrying ACCEPTED is recorded as its sender having
# accepted by the C, beside the ready itself, before the main dispatch;
# that record retires the re-send toward the sender.  A (ready, v)
# arriving without RECEIVED is its sender's word that it does not hold
# this process's accept.  Once this process has an accept to carry, it
# answers that arrival: its (ready, v), re-sent to the sender, carrying
# ACCEPTED, and RECEIVED where the sender's own accept is recorded
# here.  A (ready, v) carrying RECEIVED is answered by nothing, so an
# exchange ends in two: the answer to an unmarked arrival carries
# ACCEPTED, so the sender's answer to that, given only where it lacked
# RECEIVED and the sender has accepted, carries RECEIVED.  A lost
# answer is answered again on its target's next unmarked re-send,
# which is the loss recovery.
# Before this process accepts it has no accept to carry and answers
# nothing: the sender's unmarked re-sends keep arriving, and the first
# after the accept is answered -- the one that fires the accept
# included, which is why "accepted" here is read after the main
# dispatch's step 3 is applied, as bracha87Fig3.dtc reads it, and not
# "have accepted" at the event's start.  An unmarked re-send is a
# duplicate (ready, v); this row runs on a duplicate, so it sees it.
#
# The answer is one message, to the sender of the arrival that asked
# for it, on that arrival: no record outlives the event, and nothing
# waits for this process's next sweep.  A forged unmarked (ready, v)
# costs this process one marked (ready, v) per forged arrival, sent
# back to the forger: one message out for one in, at the forger's
# rate, and no record of what is owed a correct process changes.  A
# caller that bounds its egress chooses the order in which it serves
# answers and re-sends.
#
# Inputs:
#   "the arrival is (ready, v)"      { yes, no }
#                                    the event is the arrival of a
#                                    (ready, v); RECEIVED is read only
#                                    then
#   "carries RECEIVED"               { yes, no }
#                                    the (ready, v) carries the sender's
#                                    RECEIVED
#   "accepted"                       { yes, no }
#                                    Figure 1's, after this event: have
#                                    accepted, or step 3 taken on it
#
# Output:
#   "re-send (ready, v) to the sender"  { yes, no }

@"re-send (ready, v) to the sender","the arrival is (ready, v)","carries RECEIVED","accepted"
yes,yes,no,yes
no,yes,no,no
no,yes,yes,
no,no,,

# The echo's answer.  HOLDING rides the echo and the ready, and both
# retire: the echo at this process's accept, the ready once the
# ACCEPTED exchange is done.  A process that comes to hold p's
# (initial, v) and BESIDE only after that has nothing left to carry
# its word, and p re-sends to it until abandon.  So p's (initial, v)
# or BESIDE arriving at a process that holds both and had echoed
# before it came is p's word that it has not heard, and is answered:
# this process's (echo, v), re-sent to p, carrying HOLDING.  The
# arrival that completes the holding, or fires the echo, is not that
# word: the echo it fires carries HOLDING itself.  The same shape as
# the ready's answer: the message that asks is the one that was sent.
#
# Inputs:
#   "the arrival is (initial, v) or BESIDE"  { yes, no }  from p, as the
#                                    C has already checked
#   "holds both"                     { yes, no }  (initial, v) and
#                                    BESIDE from p, after this event
#   "have echoed"                    { yes, no }  Figure 1's, before this
#                                    event
#
# Output:
#   "re-send (echo, v) to p"         { yes, no }

@"re-send (echo, v) to p","the arrival is (initial, v) or BESIDE","holds both","have echoed"
yes,yes,yes,yes
no,yes,yes,no
no,yes,no,
no,no,,
